            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0692" n="660" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v17/pg660"/>

                <head>348. Memorandum of a Telephone Conversation Between the President and the
                    Secretary of State, Washington, June 27, 1957, 7:09 p.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d348-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1957-06-27T19:09:00-04:00"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">June 27, 1957, 7:09
                    p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">TELEPHONE CALL FROM THE PRESIDENT</p>
                <p>[Here follow Eisenhower’s comments on his choice to fill the post of Secretary of
                    Defense.]</p>
                <p>The Sec said he has the note re Saud.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d348-f2"/> We are in a
                    bad way and the Sec has been thinking about it and working on it. Saud is acting
                    as the head of the Moslem religion and not as head of state. The route through
                    the Gulf of Aqaba does not go through Saudi water. It is Egyptian and they are
                    not kicking. The Pres said he understands. Saud, said the Sec, talks in terms of
                    Arabs and Holy Places. The Pres asked if he suggested he would allow it to go
                    before the World Court. <pb facs="0693" n="661" xml:id="pg661"/> The Sec said we
                    got an intimation from some Aramco people he is getting an opinion from Manley
                    Hudson and somebody else as to how they thought it would come out. The Pres said
                    we said from the beginning we would go to the Court. The Pres thinks the
                        note<anchor n="3" xml:id="d348-f3"/> would say here is one place where we
                    don’t see eye-to-eye and this is with great regret but we have looked at the
                    thing and the entrance of the Gulf of Aqaba seems to be on the Egyptian side.
                    Because it was over and we thought it would not disturb you much and also
                    because we have always said we were ready to take it to the Court and since he
                    is concerned about the travel of the pilgrims we content ourselves at the moment
                    that any agreement of which we are a part will guarantee freedom of use by the
                    Arabs and put it before the Court. … The Près would like to get in a plane and
                    see him [Saud] if it would not create a storm. …</p>
                <p>[Here follow Dulles’ comments on his testimony before the Senate Foreign
                    Relations Committee that day.]</p>
            </div>
